{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-5424",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Since ${a}_{3}=6$ is two jumps after ${a}_{1},$ we can determine the first term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & ={a}_{1}+2d \\\\ 6 & ={a}_{1}+2(-5) \\\\ 6 & ={a}_{1}-10 \\\\ 16 & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =16+(n-1)(-5) \\\\  & =16-5n+5 \\\\  & =21-5n\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
